{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>To a foreigner you may lend at interest.</b> He forbade interest from Israel even as a positive commandment. And the opinion of the Rambam, z\"l, is that this is a positive commandment, and he counted it among the 248 positive commandments. And there are those who interpreted 'to a foreigner you may lend at interest' as not being a commandment but a permission, like (Exodus 20:9) 'Six days you shall labor,' and their intent is that the Torah did not establish a commandment in this matter in any case to lend to a non-Jew at interest, but rather the permission is in his hand whether he wishes or not, for it did not forbid him. And what they said in tractate Makkot (on Psalms 15:5) 'his money he did not give at interest' — even interest of a non-Jew — one should not understand from this a prohibition, but rather it is a fence and a stringency for one who conducts himself with those qualities and fences himself with fences and safeguards, that he may merit those virtues mentioned in that psalm. And the Rabbis, z\"l, already permitted in explicit terms interest from a non-Jew for his livelihood; this is what they said in Bava Metzia: it is forbidden to lend at interest to a gentile for more than his livelihood unless he is a Torah scholar — a decree lest he learn from his deeds. And what they said in the Sifrei, 'to a foreigner you may lend at interest' — this is a positive commandment — is not to mean that the Torah established in this a positive commandment, but rather in contrast to one who lends at interest to his fellow Israelite they said thus: that one who lends to an Israelite at interest transgresses the positive commandment of 'to a foreigner you may lend at interest,' for thus 'to a foreigner you may lend at interest' implies — and not to an Israelite; and a negative prohibition that derives from the scope of a positive commandment is reckoned as a positive commandment. And similar to this positive commandment they interpreted in the Sifrei concerning the matter of fish (Leviticus 11): 'These you may eat' — this is a positive commandment. 'And whatever does not have' — this is a negative commandment. Now is he obligated to eat pure fish? Rather, the intent is that if he ate impure fish he transgressed the positive commandment of 'these you may eat' and the negative commandment of 'and whatever does not have'; and so too here, regarding interest, it comes to teach about one who lends to an Israelite at interest that he transgresses the positive commandment and the negative commandment of 'to a foreigner you may lend at interest' — that is, 'and not to your brother' — and a negative prohibition that derives from the scope of a positive commandment is reckoned as a positive commandment; and the negative commandment of 'you shall not lend at interest to your brother.' And the Rabbis, z\"l, interpreted: 'you shall not lend at interest to your brother' — one who does the deed of your brother; this includes converts, who are forbidden to you to lend at interest. And to exclude the seed of Esau, for even though it is written of him (Numbers 20:14) 'thus says your brother Israel,' afterward it was permitted, for thus the prophet mentioned (Obadiah 1:11): 'In the day you stood aloof, in the day strangers carried off his wealth, and foreigners entered his gates and cast lots over Jerusalem — you too were as one of them.' This teaches you that the brotherhood was already annulled and it departed from its category, and he is like the rest of the foreign nations, for interest was not forbidden to Israel except for the reason that we were commanded to sustain him and to perform deeds of lovingkindness with him, as it is written (Leviticus 25:36) 'and your brother shall live with you.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:47.162053996Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}